[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"The Three Pillars of Scrum: A Technical Tutorial"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Scrum is founded on empirical process control, or empiricism. Empiricism asserts that knowledge comes from experience and making decisions based on what is observed. To implement this effectively in software engineering environments, Scrum rests on three pillars: Transparency, Inspection, and Adaptation."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Pillar 1: Transparency"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Transparency requires that aspects of the process must be visible to those responsible for the outcome. This involves a common language referring to the process and shared definitions across the development team and stakeholders."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A shared Definition of Done (DoD) to ensure everyone agrees on what quality means."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Visible tracking of work items via Kanban boards, burn-down charts, and product backlogs."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Pillar 2: Inspection"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Scrum artifacts and the progress toward agreed goals must be inspected frequently and diligently to detect undesirable variances. Inspection should not be so frequent that it gets in the way of the work."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"function evaluateSprintProgress(completedPoints, totalPoints) {
    const variance = (completedPoints / totalPoints) * 100;
    if (variance < 80) {
        triggerInspectionAlert("Potential delay detected in Sprint Backlog items.");
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Pillar 3: Adaptation"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"If an inspector determines that one or more aspects of a process deviate outside acceptable limits, and that the resulting product will be unacceptable, the process or the material being produced must be adjusted. An adjustment must be made as soon as possible to minimize further deviation."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Refactoring the CI/CD pipeline if deployment scripts fail during a Sprint."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adjusting team velocity estimations during Sprint Planning based on previous telemetry."},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"By actively maintaining Transparency, executing rigorous Inspections, and enabling rapid Adaptation, engineering teams can build resilient software systems and continuously improve their delivery processes."}]
